  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday) \nIssued at 135 AM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe main upper high center will remain east of NM Tuesday through\nFriday, though the ridge axis will continue to extend back\nwestward into NM through the period. A stronger upper low off the\ncoast of northern CA will increase southwest flow over NM. This\nflow will usher in significantly drier air above 600 mb across\nwestern NM and to some extent across central NM. This will disrupt\nor narrow the monsoon plume to be mainly over the Central Mtn\nChain. Storm coverage should reduce across western NM, but should\nincrease slightly along and just east of the Central Mtn Chain.\nThe drier air will push farther into NM on Wednesday, disrupting\nthe plume and lowering precipitation chances further. Will need to\nmonitor these two days around Ruidoso. Though it's not a obvious\nor classic pattern for flooding there, can see some slow moving\nstorms potentially causing some issues. Moisture advection should\nresume on Thursday, though modestly. This should allow for a few\nmore storms than on Wednesday. All eyes will then be back on the\nupper high which seems to have an affinity for NM this year. Both\noperational models and their ensemble counterparts show the upper\nhigh moving toward NM on Friday and centering over NE NM on\nSaturday at around 595-597 dam. It would seem we are not done \nwith the heat, yet. But before that happens, we might see some\nmoisture peel off a tropical system southwest of the Baja \nPeninsula, temporarily increasing chances for precipitation\nFriday. Fingers crossed.",
